Coding standards have been ingrained into my programming journey since the beginning. My first use of a rigorous implementation of coding anaylsis software was through JUnit tests in Java. ESLint with VSCode is another code analysis plugin that makes it extremely useful to improve your code's quality and readability. Furthermore, having a code checker gives programmers a firm foundation on coding standards and could infact help them better learn the language's syntax. After my first week of using ESlint and VSCode, I believe it's an extremely useful tool in software engineering because it sets a baseline standard for readable code, improves uniformity and structure, and sustains maintainability. 

One of the biggest benefits of implementing coding standards is that they make the actual code easier to read and understand. All programmers write code in many different ways which may become difficult to read and understand it. Things such as indentation, spacing, naming conventions, and how methods/functions are structured may seem like miniscule details, but they can make a huge difference when analyzing a larger program. ESLint helps to enforce many of these standards automatically, which means that programmers do not have to rely on remebering every rule themselves. This is especially helpful when working on a project with a team since everyone can follow the same basic style. 

I also found that ESLint can help me better learn and understand Javascript and Typescript. At first, fixing ESLint errors can be frustrating because some of the errors seem like minor issues that do not hamper the programs execution. However, looking at these errors has actually helped me understand why certain coding practices are preferred. For example, ESLint can point out unused variables, length of code segments, and other problems that I may not have noticed on my own. Therefore, I can not only fix my error, but I can also internalize what caused it and avoid making the same mistake in the future. In this way, ESLint acts as more than just making code look pretty. It can also be a valuable learning tool. 

Overall, having working with ESLint so far, I believe that coding standards are more important than they might initally seem. While certain rules about spacing and formatting may appear to be trivial, they ultimately contribute to making software easier to read, maintain, and collaborate on. My first few weeks of using ESLint was a double-edged sword. I has shown me that getting rid of errors can be both painful and useful. Some errors may have felt unnecessary at first but following the standards has made me more aware of how I write code. As I continue learning to program, I think tools like ESLint will help me devlop better habits and write code that is easier for both myself and other programmers to understand. 





